    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class, and the full suite is green (2/2 passing, `BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## Summary\n\nThe change lives entirely in `Owner.getLocality()` \u2014 the derived, non-persisted region accessor.\n\n**What I changed** (`src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Owner.java`):\n\n1. Added a `REGION_POSTCODES` table mapping each region to its inclusive 4-digit range (NSW 2000\u20132099, VIC 3000\u20133099, QLD 4000\u20134099).\n2. Rewrote `getLocality()` to consult the postcode first via a new `regionForPostcode(...)` helper, falling back to the existing `CITY_REGION` city table (and finally `\"UNKNOWN\"`) only when the postcode is absent or in no known range.\n\n**Behavior:**\n- Known cities with an in-region postcode still return the same region (`Sydney` + `2000` \u2192 `NSW`).\n- A postcode now disambiguates: an unknown city with a `3000` postcode \u2192 `VIC`, proving postcode precedence.\n- Absent/malformed/out-of-range postcodes fall back to the city table exactly as before, so no existing rule regresses. The controller's `postcodeValidForCity` check remains consistent since a valid in-range postcode makes `getLocality()` return that same region.\n\nBoth tests (`coreRegionFromPostcodeRange`, `functionalityPostcodePreferredOverCity`) pass.",
